WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.190
MAX: You've got an
awesome new app,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.857
and now you need to
think about securing

00:00:03.857 --> 00:00:05.620
it and your user's data.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.770
Whether you made an app
about time travel, kittens,

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.630
or hover bikes, you're probably
asking the same question.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:12.750
Is my app secure?

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.970
CARTER: But like how every
application is unique,

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.610
securing applications
is just as unique.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.130
In this video, you'll see
the example that we created

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.260
and how Google Cloud
helped us secure it.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.417
MAX: Security isn't a
one-size-fits-all solution.

00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:26.250
For example, if you're
making a banking app,

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.570
you're going to have different
concerns than someone

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.925
making a photo-sharing app.

00:00:28.925 --> 00:00:30.300
CARTER: But we
can't show you how

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.310
to secure an app
we haven't seen,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.980
so instead, we created
Add a Cat to That

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.350
to show you how Google Cloud
helps you secure your apps.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.380
MAX: How do you use this
fantastic application?

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.088
Start with an awesome
photo, like the one

00:00:42.088 --> 00:00:43.793
Jen Tong took in San Francisco.

00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:45.210
You send it up to
the cloud, where

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:51.660
we do sophisticated artificial
intelligence to add cats.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.450
Now we send that photo
back to you, improved.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.460
And it even works
recursively because everyone

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.490
knows the internet
benefits from more cats.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.280
And voila, your photo is better.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.880
CARTER: So how did we make it
all happen behind the scenes?

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.890
It's actually a very
short Python program--

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.720
about 20 lines of
code-- implemented

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.710
using the Flask framework.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.260
Once we get the
photo, we kick it

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.570
over to ImageMagick that
adds a cat to the photo,

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.570
and we take the result and
send it back to our cat lovers,

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.325
better than it was before.

00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:22.200
MAX: Next, we had to
think about, how were we

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.900
going to run the software?

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.650
CARTER: We deploy this
inside a container,

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.270
a lightweight and
portable executable

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.250
image that contains software
in all its dependencies.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.250
This lets us speed up
development and deployment

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.072
to production.

00:01:33.072 --> 00:01:34.530
MAX: Since we're
using a container,

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.170
we're able to use Cloud
Run to click a button,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.660
and that creates the image,
runs the image in production,

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.130
and points our domain at it.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.325
So the app can serve cat
images to real humans.

00:01:43.325 --> 00:01:44.700
CARTER: Now we're
up and running.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.830
And the question that
always gets asked,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.990
is Add a Cat to That secure?

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:49.490
Hmm.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.792
Now that's a little
more complicated.

00:01:51.792 --> 00:01:54.000
MAX: Even with an app as
simple as Add a Cat to That,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.387
we've got a lot of work to
do to keep our application

00:01:56.387 --> 00:01:58.470
from becoming one of those
security horror stories

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.240
you read about in the headlines.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.530
CARTER: So what do you think?

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.510
Can you see any potential
vulnerabilities?

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.470
Let us know in the
comments below.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.039
And in the next video, we'll
show you what we fixed.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.030
I'm Carter.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:09.838
This is Max.

00:02:09.838 --> 00:02:11.880
MAX: Thanks for watching
and for adding more cats

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:12.505
to your photos.

00:02:12.505 --> 00:02:15.530
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

